- op: discuss  # 73f4
  at: '2026-09-01T20:57:30Z'  # 73f4
  by: agent  # 73f4
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 73f4
  clock: 4  # 73f4
  nonce: 73f4  # 73f4
  message: "DONE in PR #692, merged to dev as ac0d3b881f. ADR-0051 lands the module-key\naxis: the ADR, module_key_axis.py, \"module-key\" wired into _known_axes(), the\nir.py declaration retired, and tests/test_module_key_axis.py (12 tests).\n19,742 tests pass, 100% coverage, mypy ratchet clean.\n\nVERIFIED ON THE MERGED TREE WITH THE PREMISE AS A LIVE CONTROL:\n  module-key in _known_axes           True; 6 notions\n  axiom-conformant                    namespace, type  (derived from section)\n  global_object conformant            False  (\"not yet argued\" != accepted)\n  unknown notion conformant           False  (default is the safe direction)\n  ir.py declaration                   module_path: str  # axis: module-key\n  false clause gone                   True\n  find_field_drift on live tree       none\n  CONTROL: _module_matches still branches on capitalisation -> True\nThe control matters: if that ever stops being true, `free-text` becomes\ndefensible again and this axis should be RE-ARGUED rather than silently kept.\nIt is pinned as a test, not just checked here.\n\nTHE ITEM'S HEADLINE PREMISE HELD AND WAS SHARPER THAN FILED. The module key did\nnot lack a declaration; ExternalRef.module_path CARRIED one, and it was false --\n\"consumers display/lookup, never branch on the value itself\" against a\n_module_matches that decides type-vs-sub-package from orthography. The lint\npassed it because a free-text justification must be PRESENT, not TRUE. So this\nretired a wrong axis as much as it added a right one.\n\nTHE AXIOM, and the one judgment call inside it: a TYPE IS CONFORMANT. The\ncatalogues are deliberately full of types (net.Conn, std::fs::File,\npathlib.Path) because a method-shaped primitive is unaddressable without its\nowning type, and IoPrimitive.module's docstring has always said \"module or class\npath\". What is NOT conformant is a receiver VARIABLE -- a local binding is not\nwhere the callee is defined. That is ADR-0023's endpoint-property cut reused,\nand the tell that it is the right cut is that _module_matches already carries a\nSwift carve-out whose only purpose is to tolerate the non-conformant shape.\n\nTWO FINDINGS THAT CAME OUT OF THE WORK, both recorded on their own items rather\nthan acted on here:\n  * INV-kotob's SWIFT EMISSION SITE IS LOCATED (swift.py:1223) -- the audit had\n    it as \"objc located, swift's not\". It is a four-clause fallback yielding a\n    TYPE, a NAMESPACE, a receiver VARIABLE and a SENTINEL into one slot. That is\n    now the best single piece of evidence for this axiom, better than the corpus\n    percentages because it is four lines of source.\n  * THE AUDIT MIS-ATTRIBUTED THE RECEIVER-VARIABLE NOTION TO OBJC. objc.py:644\n    is gated on receiver_name[0].isupper(), and a capitalised Objective-C\n    message receiver is normally a CLASS name, so objc's slot is predominantly a\n    TYPE and conformant. The lowercase identifiers the audit counted came from\n    swift; \"26% of objc+swift refs\" is a combined-cohort figure. WI-marok's\n    migration target was corrected.\n\nDEVIATIONS FROM ADR-0024, STATED RATHER THAN QUIETLY SKIPPED:\n  * NO DRIFT LINTER. Enforcement is mandatory under the template, and this axis\n    ships with a property test only: the AST walker checks a consumer's\n    hardcoded set against a registry, and a structural-policy axis has no\n    enumerable value set to check. The live enforcement is the `# axis:`\n    declaration lint on core dataclasses, which is what the ir.py change\n    satisfies and which now passes on the whole tree.\n  * NOTHING AN ANALYZER EMITS CHANGES. external_symbol ids embed the slot, so\n    migration needs the v9/v10 scheme bump WI-talos gates. ADR-0024 step 7 =\n    WI-marok. Until then the axiom describes what the field SHOULD hold while\n    7.8% of shipped slots do not -- a documented gap, not a fixed one.\n\nOPEN QUESTIONS RECORDED, NEITHER ANSWERED: global_object (process/window/\nnavigator) is genuinely contested -- you do not import `process` in node, so the\nglobal's name is arguably how JS spells that owner path -- and is left for a\nper-value audit with NO ROW MOVED. ExternalRef.name carries the SAME clause just\nproven false on its sibling while strip_redundant_module_qualifier decomposes it\nwith rpartition(\".\"); filed as WI-biguz rather than folded in.\n\nWI-livar stays open as the campaign's design home: WI-mubup (widen\nDEFAULT_CORE_FILES to io_boundary.py) is now UNBLOCKED, since both axes exist."  # 73f4
